{
  "outcome": "needs_user_info",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi James, sorry about this, especially with your shift starting soon. I checked and your warehouse office desktop isn't connected to our network right now. Its disk and health look fine, so this is most likely a connection problem. Please try these steps: 1) Make sure the network cable is plugged in firmly at the back of the PC and at the wall or floor port. Unplug it and plug it back in, and check that the small light next to the port comes on. 2) If the PC uses Wi-Fi, turn Wi-Fi off and back on, or forget the network and reconnect. 3) Restart the desktop fully (Start > Power > Restart). 4) If you can, try a different cable or wall port. Please reply and let me know: are other computers or colleagues in the warehouse office also without network, or is it only your desktop? And do you see any error message or a red X on the network icon? If others are affected too, we'll treat this as an office network outage and pass it straight to our network team.",
  "internal_note": "User: James O'Connor (id 8), Warehouse Supervisor, active account, no lockout or sign-in block. Device BL-DT-0108 (Windows 11 desktop): offline, last check-in 2026-10-09T12:28:32Z, 78% disk free, no warnings. This confirms the device has no path to the network. Followed KB-014 quick checks; there is no KB article for wired desktops. I've sent the user self-help steps (cable/link light, Wi-Fi toggle, restart, different port or cable) and asked if colleagues nearby are affected. Next step: if several warehouse office users are down, escalate to Tier 2 as an office network outage (P1/P2 depending on how many are blocked). If only this desktop is affected and the steps fail, escalate for an on-site check of the NIC, cable or switch port. User is blocked and their shift starts in about 1 hour, so please follow up quickly.",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-014"
  ],
  "confidence": "medium"
}
